abstract:
I am using rustgpu, which is a rather experimental tool to compile rust into shaders. I have been noticing that for many models, even though I see implementations of sphere tracing rendering with no issue, I get problems with mine, but afaict they should be the same. 
 For example consider this glsl code taken from  shadertoy . 

#define ITER 1024
float march(vec3 rayOriginWS, vec3 rayDirWS, float rand, out vec3 sceneColour, out float responsiveness)
{       
    vec4 material;

    vec3 inscatter = oz.yyy;
    float transmittance = 1.0;

    float t = 0.001;
    float d;

    for(int i = NON_CONST_ZERO; i < ITER; ++i)
    {   
        vec3 posWS = rayOriginWS + rayDirWS*t;
        d = fSDF(posWS, kRenderFilter, iChannel2, material);

        t += d;


        if(d < 0.0001)
        {
            break;
        }

    }

    sceneColour = computeFinalLighting(t, rayOriginWS, rayDirWS, material);

    sceneColour = sceneColour * transmittance + inscatter;


    return t;
}

 It produces a beautiful image of a tree alongside grass, mountains, the things is outright gorgeous. 
 I copied and transpiled the same sdf code, and tried to replicate just the tree part on my end. With this sphere tracing code: 
 pub fn trace_sdf(
    start: &Vec3,
    ray: &Vec3,
    mut sdf: impl FnMut(&Vec3, usize, f32) -> f32,
    min_step: f32,
    max_step: f32,
    cutoff: f32,
    iter_max: usize,
    mut termination: impl FnMut(&Vec3, f32) -> bool,
) -> (bool, Vec3, usize, bool, f32)
{
    let mut iter_count = 0;
    let mut hit_sdf = false;
    let mut current_pos = start.clone();
    let mut prior_d = f32::MAX;
    let mut terminated = false;
    let mut distance = 0.0001;

    let mut small_step_count = 0;
    while iter_count < iter_max
    {
        current_pos = *start + distance * ray;
        let increment = sdf(&current_pos, iter_count, prior_d);
        distance += increment.clamp(min_step, max_step);
        if increment <= cutoff
        {
            hit_sdf = true;
            break;
        }

        iter_count += 1
    }

    (hit_sdf, current_pos, iter_count, terminated, distance)
}

 If I call the above like this: 

    let (v_collision, v_collision_point, _, _, _) = trace_sdf(
        &cam_pos,
        &ray,
        tree_sdf,
        0.0001,
        0.001, // we are capping the max distance we can travel by, by a lot.
        0.05,
        1600,
        |_, _| false,
    );

 I do get an image 

 However, notice that the above is effectively ray marching instead of sphere tracing, since the maximum step is very clamped. If I instead allow for large steps, by making that upper bound much larger, say even just 1, I get this instead: 

 As if I am tracing way past collision. This seems to happen because I am multiplying the input position point by 10 as a way to scale down the sdf model. 
 The cause of the problem 
 I don;t fully understand why a scalar constant would affect the result of raytracing so much. 
 I.e. this breaks the tracing logic: 
     let tree_sdf = |v: &Vec3, _: usize, _: f32| {
        let mut tmp = Vec4::default();
        f_tree_sdf(*v * 10., 0., 0., &mut tmp).powf(1.)
    };

 And I don;t get why.
